      "description": "Signature: every plan a cell; red where ruled out, yellow where chosen.",
      "html": "<div class=\"ds-board\"><span class=\"ds-pc\"></span><span class=\"ds-pc ds-blk\">ATC</span><span class=\"ds-pc ds-blk ds-t2\">AIR</span><span class=\"ds-pc ds-safe\"></span><span class=\"ds-pc ds-chosen\">&rarr;</span></div>",
      "css": ":root{--ground:#dfe2e4;--surface:#f7f8f8;--ink:#141517;--rule:#c6cbce;--rule-strong:#858c91;--sign-black:#141414;--sign-yellow:#f5c400;--sign-red:#c4122f;--sign-white:#fff;--go:#1d6b3a;--yellow-tint:#fbf0c2}.ds-board{display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(5,40px);gap:3px}.ds-pc{height:34px;border-radius:1px;display:grid;place-items:center;background:var(--sign-white);box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 1px var(--rule);font:700 11px/1 'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;transition:background 300ms,box-shadow 300ms}.ds-pc:hover{outline:2px solid var(--ink);outline-offset:1px}.ds-blk{background:var(--sign-red);color:var(--sign-white);box-shadow:none;font-size:10px}.ds-t2{box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 3px var(--sign-black)}.ds-safe{box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 2px var(--go)}.ds-chosen{background:var(--sign-yellow);color:var(--sign-black);box-shadow:inset 0 0 0 2px var(--sign-black)}"
    }
  ],
  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Airside Sign Line",
    "overview": "The interface reads like the signage an aircraft taxis past: a cool concrete ground, and on it a small set of panels whose colour tells you, before you read a word, what kind of statement they make. Black panels with yellow type say where you are. Yellow panels with black type and an arrow say which way to go. Red panels with white type say stop: a hard limit or a rejection. Everything else is plain, quiet, and ruled with 1px lines.\n\nDensity is high but legible at laptop distance. The condensed sign face carries every label, identifier, heading and button; the plain Barlow body carries explanation, always in sentence case. Numbers are tabular everywhere so turns, minutes and scores line up across columns. Corners are square (2px), so panels feel fabricated rather than soft.\n\nThe world refuses the generic card dashboard where every box weighs the same. Weight comes from sign colour, not from size or decoration, and the most important object on screen is the one yellow direction sign.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Cool concrete ground (ground) with near-white surfaces and 1px cool-grey rules.",
      "Three sign colours, each with one job: black for location, yellow for direction, red for mandatory.",
      "A fourth, quieter signal, signal green (go), only for signed and verified states.",
      "Barlow Condensed uppercase for panels and labels, Barlow for prose, tabular numerals throughout.",
      "Square 2px corners; plan-board cells at 1px.",
      "Motion only to advance the story: turns reveal in sequence while the location sign moves."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The One Job Rule",
        "body": "Each sign colour has exactly one meaning: black-and-yellow is location, yellow is direction, red is mandatory, green is signed. Never use red for emphasis, yellow for decoration, or green for \"nice\".",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The One Yellow Sign Rule",
        "body": "At most one solid yellow direction sign carries the recommendation on a screen; the primary button and the chosen plan cell repeat the same plan, never a competing one.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Not Only Colour Rule",
        "body": "Every signal colour travels with text or an icon (party code in a blocked cell, \"Signed\", \"Rejected\", arrow on direction).",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Painted Panel Rule",
        "body": "Uppercase condensed type is for things a sign would say (names, IDs, states, actions). Sentences are never uppercase and never condensed.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Tabular Rule",
        "body": "`font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` is set on the body and inherited everywhere.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Painted Not Lifted Rule",
        "body": "Nothing rises on hover. State is shown by a colour change, a keyline, or a 2px outline, never by a larger shadow or a lift.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do give each signal colour one job: black-and-yellow for location, yellow for direction, red for mandatory, green for signed.",
      "Do set every label, ID, state and action in Barlow Condensed uppercase, and every sentence in Barlow sentence case.",
